.Sh DESCRIPTION
The library behind
.Xr pcompress 1 .
A
.Vt pc_ctx_t
holds the settings for one job; the job turns a stream into the
.Pa .pz
format or back.
Several contexts may run at the same time in different threads of one
process.
One context must be used by one thread at a time.
The library installs no signal handlers and never exits the process.
.Pp
Every path given to the library is UTF-8, on every platform, and archive
entry names are stored as UTF-8.
On Windows the library opens the files it names itself
.Pq Fn pc_index_open
through the UTF-16 calls; a program using the command-line style API
there should also make UTF-8 its code page with an application manifest
.Pq Li activeCodePage ,
as
.Nm pcompress.exe
does.
.Pp
.Fn pc_new
returns a context with default settings: zstd, level 6 (lz4: level 1),
a chunk size chosen from the level, one thread per core, the BLAKE256
chunk checksum, and no deduplication or encryption.
.Fn pc_free
releases it and wipes any stored password.
A context can run any number of jobs; each job starts from its settings,
not from what the previous job left behind.
.Ss Settings
Each setter returns 0, or \-1 with
.Dv PC_ERR_ARGS
recorded for
.Fn pc_last_error .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fn pc_set_algorithm
One of
.Qq zstd ,
.Qq lz4 ,
.Qq lzma ,
.Qq lzmaMt ,
.Qq bzip2 ,
.Qq zlib ,
.Qq ppmd ,
.Qq lzfx ,
.Qq libbsc ,
.Qq adapt ,
.Qq adapt2
or
.Qq none .
The name is copied.
.It Fn pc_set_level
1 (fastest) to 14 (smallest output).
.It Fn pc_set_chunk_size
The unit of parallelism, in bytes; 0 picks one from the level.
Deduplication needs at least 1 MB.
.It Fn pc_set_threads
0 means one per core.
.It Fn pc_set_checksum
.Qq BLAKE256 ,
.Qq BLAKE512 ,
.Qq SHA256 ,
.Qq SHA512 ,
.Qq KECCAK256 ,
.Qq KECCAK512 ,
.Qq SKEIN256 ,
.Qq SKEIN512
or
.Qq CRC64 .
.It Fn pc_set_dedupe
.Dv PC_DEDUPE_NONE ,
.Dv PC_DEDUPE_RABIN
(content-defined blocks within a chunk),
.Dv PC_DEDUPE_FIXED
(fixed blocks) or
.Dv PC_DEDUPE_GLOBAL .
Global deduplication needs a seekable output and is refused by
.Fn pc_compress_fd .
.It Fn pc_set_encryption
.Qq AES
or
.Qq SALSA20
in CTR mode with an HMAC per chunk;
.Dv NULL
turns encryption off.
.Fa keylen
is 16 or 32 bytes, 0 for 32.
.It Fn pc_set_password
The password, used by the next job; all of it feeds the key derivation
(scrypt).
It is copied; the caller's buffer is not changed.
.El
.Ss Jobs
.Fn pc_compress_fd
reads
.Fa in_fd
to end of file and writes a
.Pa .pz
stream to
.Fa out_fd .
.Fn pc_decompress_fd
does the reverse and checks every chunk's checksum, or its HMAC if the
stream is encrypted.
Neither function closes its descriptors.
Both return 0 on success and \-1 on failure.
The output of a failed job is incomplete and must not be used.
.Pp
Streams written by these functions are the same as
.Ql pcompress -p
output, and either side can read the other's.
Archives
.Pq Fl a
and files written with global deduplication need
.Xr pcompress 1
or the command-line style API.
.Ss Progress
.Fn pc_set_progress
has
.Fn pc_compress_fd ,
.Fn pc_decompress_fd
and
.Fn start_pcompress
call
.Fn fn arg in out
from the calling thread each time they hand a chunk to a worker.
.Fa in
is the input consumed so far and
.Fa out
the chunk bytes written so far, without the stream header.
If
.Fa fn
returns non-zero, the job stops: its threads are joined, what it
allocated is freed, an output file it created is removed (a caller's
descriptor is left alone, and an archive extraction keeps the files it
has written), and the call fails with
.Dv PC_ERR_CANCELLED .
A NULL
.Fa fn
removes the callback.
.Ss Diagnostics
Messages from all threads working for a context go to stderr, or to
.Fa fn
after
.Fn pc_set_log .
.Fa fn
is called as
.Fn fn arg level msg ,
never at the same time for one context.
.Fa level
is the highest level delivered:
.Dv PC_LOG_ERR ,
.Dv PC_LOG_WARN ,
.Dv PC_LOG_INFO
or
.Dv PC_LOG_VERBOSE ;
.Dv PC_LOG_NONE
delivers nothing, and \-1 uses the process default.
.Pp
.Fn pc_last_error
returns the first line of the first error in the last call on
.Fa ctx ,
or the empty string.
.Fn pc_last_error_code
returns its class:
.Bl -tag -width PC_ERR_INTERNAL -compact
.It Dv PC_OK
no error
.It Dv PC_ERR_ARGS
invalid setting or argument
.It Dv PC_ERR_IO
a read, write, open or stat failed
.It Dv PC_ERR_NOMEM
out of memory
.It Dv PC_ERR_FORMAT
not a
.Pa .pz
stream, or a newer format version
.It Dv PC_ERR_CORRUPT
damaged data
.It Dv PC_ERR_VERIFY
a checksum, CRC or HMAC did not match, or the password is wrong
.It Dv PC_ERR_CRYPTO
key setup failed or no password was given
.It Dv PC_ERR_INTERNAL
anything else
.It Dv PC_ERR_CANCELLED
the
.Fn pc_set_progress
callback stopped the job
.El
.Fn pc_strerror
names a class.
.Pp
.Fn pc_cleanup_tempfiles
removes the temporary files of all running jobs.
It is async-signal-safe, for use in a program's own signal handler
before it exits.
.Ss Chunks
Building blocks for a backup tool that stores deduplicated, encrypted
objects:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fn pc_chunker_new
A content-defined chunker: boundaries come from a rolling hash of the
data, so equal data gives equal chunks wherever it occurs, and an edit
changes only the chunks around it.
.Fa avg
(rounded down to a power of two),
.Fa min
and
.Fa max
are in bytes; 0 picks 64 KB, avg/4 and avg*4.
.It Fn pc_chunker_new2
The same, with a choice of boundary function:
.Dv PC_CHUNKER_RABIN
(that of
.Fn pc_chunker_new )
or
.Dv PC_CHUNKER_GEAR
(FastCDC's gear hash with normalized chunking, several times faster, with
chunk sizes near min + avg).
Either one's boundaries never change between releases; a repository must
record which one it uses and keep it.
.It Fn pc_chunker_feed
Takes any amount of input and appends the length of each chunk that ends
in it to
.Fa lens ,
returning how many.
The result does not depend on how the input is split across calls.
Size
.Fa lens
as len / min + 1.
.Fn pc_chunker_finish
returns the last chunk.
.It Fn pc_chunk_id
BLAKE2b-256 of a chunk, keyed when
.Fa key
is given, so that object names do not reveal content hashes.
.It Fn pc_set_key
A raw 32-byte key for sealed objects.
.It Fn pc_derive_key
A 32-byte key for one named purpose from a master key.
Derive separate keys for chunk ids and for sealing (for example
.Qq chunk-id
and
.Qq seal )
instead of using the master key for both; the names are the caller's to
choose and keep stable.
.It Fn pc_chunk_seal
Compresses one chunk with the context's algorithm and level, stores it
raw if it does not shrink, and with a key encrypts it (XSalsa20, random
nonce) and authenticates header and payload (keyed BLAKE2b-256).
Without a key the object has an unkeyed BLAKE2b-256 checksum.
The output buffer needs
.Fn pc_chunk_seal_bound len
bytes.
.It Fn pc_chunk_open
Checks the tag before decrypting or decompressing, then restores the
chunk.
A changed or truncated object fails with
.Dv PC_ERR_VERIFY
or
.Dv PC_ERR_CORRUPT ,
a wrong key with
.Dv PC_ERR_VERIFY ,
a missing key with
.Dv PC_ERR_CRYPTO ,
and an unencrypted object while a key is set with
.Dv PC_ERR_VERIFY .
An object does not record which chunk it is: whoever controls the storage
can put a valid object under another one's name.
After opening, compare
.Fn pc_chunk_id
of the result with the name the object was fetched by.
.Fn pc_chunk_open_size
reads the plaintext size from the header (unverified) to size the buffer.
.It Fn pc_keyfile_create
Makes a random 256-bit key and writes it, wrapped with a password of any
length, to
.Fn pc_keyfile_size
bytes at
.Fa out
(scrypt N=2^17 r=8 p=1, then XSalsa20 and a BLAKE2b-256 tag).
.Fn pc_keyfile_open
recovers the key; a wrong password or any change to the file fails.
The KDF and its parameters are stored in the file, and
.Fn pc_keyfile_open
refuses parameters beyond 512 MB or unreasonable work.
.It Fn pc_index_open
Opens or creates a persistent set of 32-byte chunk ids, kept in an
append-only file, so a backup tool can skip chunks it already stored
without asking its object store.
A file that is not an index is refused and left untouched; a partial
record left by a crash is dropped.
.Fn pc_index_has
and
.Fn pc_index_add
return 1 or 0 (present or absent; added or already there) and \-1 on
error.
Adds are buffered until
.Fn pc_index_sync
or
.Fn pc_index_close ,
which write and
.Fn fsync
them.
Add an id only after its object is durably stored: then a crash can only
make the tool store a chunk twice, never skip one.
Use one thread at a time per index.
Several processes may append to one file; each sees the others' ids at its
next open.
Only a hash table is held in memory, 11 to 21 bytes per id; the ids stay
in the file, and a lookup that matches reads its id back to confirm it.
Callers with their own catalogue need not use it: look ids up there
instead.
.It Fn pc_manifest_new
A snapshot manifest: entries
.Pq Vt pc_entry_t
holding a relative path, type
.Pq Dv PC_ENTRY_FILE , PC_ENTRY_DIR , PC_ENTRY_SYMLINK , PC_ENTRY_HARDLINK ,
.Dv PC_ENTRY_CHARDEV , PC_ENTRY_BLOCKDEV , PC_ENTRY_FIFO ,
mode, owner, modification time, size, a symlink's or hard link's target,
and a file's chunk ids and lengths in order.
Since 4.4 an entry can also hold birth and change times, device and inode
numbers (for change detection), a device's
.Fa rdev ,
file flags, owner and group names, extended attributes, ACLs in their
system's text form, a sparse-file mark, and for a directory the id of
another manifest holding its contents
.Pq Fa subtree ,
so that a tool can store a tree as one manifest per directory and reuse
an unchanged directory by id.
Records written by a newer library are kept in
.Fa ext
and written back unchanged.
.Fn pc_manifest_encode
writes format 2, which 4.3 and older cannot read;
.Fn pc_manifest_decode
reads formats 1 and 2.
.Fn pc_manifest_add
copies an entry, refusing absolute paths, empty, "." and ".."
components, and chunk lengths that do not add up to the size.
Entries are kept sorted by path;
.Fn pc_manifest_get
and
.Fn pc_manifest_find
return pointers into the manifest, valid until
.Fn pc_manifest_free .
.Fn pc_manifest_encode
writes the bytes to store (with
.Fa buf
NULL it only sets
.Fa *len )
and fails on duplicate paths or an entry under a symlink entry or under
a directory with a subtree.
.Fn pc_manifest_decode
treats its input as hostile and also refuses unsorted entries and
trailing bytes, so a restore that follows a decoded manifest cannot be
steered outside its target directory through ".." or a symlink it just
created.
Seal the encoded manifest like any chunk to encrypt and authenticate it.
.Pp
.Vt pc_entry_t
may gain fields at its end in a later version.
The header maps
.Fn pc_manifest_add ,
.Fn pc_manifest_get
and
.Fn pc_manifest_find
to
.Fn pc_manifest_add_sz ,
.Fn pc_manifest_get_sz
and
.Fn pc_manifest_find_sz ,
which also take
.Li sizeof (pc_entry_t)
as the program saw it, so a program built against another version of
the header keeps working: fields one side does not know read as zero,
and add refuses an entry that sets a field the library does not know
rather than drop it.
.El
.Pp
The object format is described in
.Pa DESIGN.md .
.Pa examples/pzstore.c
is a small deduplicating store built on these calls;
.Pa examples/backup/pcbackup.c
a snapshot backup tool (backup, list, restore of everything or one file,
forget, prune).
.Ss Embedding
These set process-wide behaviour for a program that hosts the library.
Call them before anything else in the library, and before other threads
use it.
.Pp
.Fn pc_set_allocator
gives the library the host's memory functions:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef struct {
	size_t size;	/* sizeof (pc_allocator_t) */
	void *(*malloc)(void *ud, size_t n);
	void *(*realloc)(void *ud, void *p, size_t n);
	void (*free)(void *ud, void *p);
	void *ud;
} pc_allocator_t;
.Ed
.Pp
All three functions are required; library threads call them, so they
must be thread-safe.
.Fa realloc
and
.Fa free
are never passed NULL.
A NULL
.Fa a
restores the C library's functions.
Once the library has allocated anything (the first
.Fn pc_new ,
.Fn create_pc_context ,
.Fn pc_chunker_new ,
.Fn pc_index_open ,
.Fn pc_manifest_new ,
.Fn pc_manifest_decode ,
.Fn pc_keyfile_create ,
.Fn pc_chunk_seal ,
and so on), or for a struct whose
.Fa size
is too small or that lacks a function, it returns \-1 and changes
nothing.
The library's own code, the bundled zstd, lz4 and libbsc, and the
allocation hooks of lzma, PPMd, zlib and bzip2 use these functions.
libarchive (archive mode), OpenSSL (see
.Xr CRYPTO_set_mem_functions 3 ) ,
the C++ filters (packJPG, packPNM, dispack and the dictionary filter)
and the OpenMP runtime allocate on their own.
.Pp
.Fn pc_set_default_log
sets the process's default sink: messages from contexts without their
own
.Fn pc_set_log
sink, and messages logged outside any context (for example by
.Fn pc_set_allocator ) ,
go to
.Fn fn arg level msg
instead of stderr.
A NULL
.Fa fn
restores stderr.
.Fa level
is the highest level delivered to it, also for contexts whose own level
is \-1;
.Dv PC_LOG_NONE
delivers nothing and \-1 keeps the current level
.Pq Dv PC_LOG_INFO at start .
.Fa fn
is called from library threads, at the same time for different
contexts.
.Pp
.Fn pc_set_thread_hooks
runs host code in every thread the library creates:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef struct {
	size_t size;	/* sizeof (pc_thread_hooks_t) */
	void (*start)(void *ud, const char *name);
	void (*stop)(void *ud);
	void *ud;
} pc_thread_hooks_t;
.Ed
.Pp
.Fa start
is the first thing a new thread runs, with its name:
.Ql pc-compress ,
.Ql pc-decompress ,
.Ql pc-writer ,
.Ql pc-meta ,
.Ql pc-archive
or
.Ql pc-extract .
.Fa stop
is the last thing it runs before it exits.
Either may be NULL, not both; a NULL
.Fa h
removes the hooks.
It returns 0, or \-1 for a struct that is too small or has no function.
Threads that OpenMP, zstd's worker pool, lzmaMt or libbsc start are not
covered.
.Ss Command-line style API
.Fn create_pc_context ,
.Fn init_pc_context "ctx" "argc" "argv" ,
.Fn start_pcompress
and
.Fn destroy_pc_context
run a job described by
.Xr pcompress 1
arguments, with file names, archives and every option.
.Fn init_pc_context
returns 0, 1 for an invalid argument, 2 for a usage error, 3 for
.Fl h
and 4 for
.Fl V .
.Sh EXAMPLES
Compress standard input to standard output:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pcompress/pcompress.h>

int
main(void)
{
	pc_ctx_t *c = pc_new();
	int rv;

	pc_set_algorithm(c, "zstd");
	pc_set_level(c, 9);
	pc_set_log(c, NULL, NULL, PC_LOG_NONE);
	if ((rv = pc_compress_fd(c, 0, 1)) != 0)
		fprintf(stderr, "%s\en", pc_last_error(c));
	pc_free(c);
	return (rv != 0);
}
.Ed
.Pp
Build with
.Dl cc $(pkg-config --cflags libpcompress) x.c $(pkg-config --libs libpcompress)
.Pp
.Pa examples/pzcat.c
in the source tree is a complete filter;
.Pa examples/pzstore.c
a deduplicating, encrypted chunk store;
.Pa examples/backup/pcbackup.c
a snapshot backup tool.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pcompress 1
.Sh HISTORY
The descriptor API, per-context logging and error classes appeared in
pcompress 4.1; chunks, key files, the chunk index and manifests in 4.2.
The command-line style API dates from 2.x.
.Fn pc_set_allocator ,
.Fn pc_set_default_log ,
.Fn pc_set_thread_hooks
and
.Fn pc_set_progress
appeared after 4.4.
.Sh CAVEATS
Only the symbols declared in
.In pcompress/pcompress.h
are exported.
The bundled zstd, lz4, libbsc and libarchive are internal, so a program
may link its own copies.
.Pp
The adaptive-mode counters printed by
.Fl C
are shared by all contexts in a process.
